{
  "version": 1,
  "kind": "Graph",
  "name": "Freefall",
  "description": "Algorithmic: high pulses fall away abruptly by a chaotic amount on a motion-driven clock — never the same twice",
  "author": "ShockingVRC",
  "graph": {
    "version": 1,
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 1,
        "pos": [
          40.0,
          -280.0
        ],
        "title": "Algorithmic · freefall",
        "kind": {
          "type": "Frame",
          "size": [
            720.0,
            250.0
          ],
          "text": "A high pulse rate (70–100 Hz) that keeps falling away — abruptly, and by a different amount every time.\nWhen: the Drop clock fires at a tempo set by depth, velocity, acceleration and recoil plus a little noise; depth makes each drop stay low longer, acceleration shorter.\nHow far: every beat iterates a logistic map x → r·x·(1 − x) in its chaotic range. Its growth r (3.57–4.0) is set by all four values, so the depths never repeat and a slightly different stroke sends them somewhere new.\nHow it moves: the fall takes one or two 25 ms ticks — recoil makes it snappier — while the intensity sinks more slowly, faster with velocity and deeper after a recoil peak. Acceleration and x set how fast it climbs back. Depth raises the top intensity; minutes of fast strokes heat the top frequency down by up to 16 Hz.\nSliders: Max frequency drop caps every fall (Hz). Max device power is Limit Max on both channels — 6 to start; the Graph cap in Setup still wins. B follows A 160 ms later."
        }
      },
